"EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16523)\nciteturn104academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 days ago; Across three interactive-agent benchmarks and two backbone models, EAU consistently outperforms prior RL methods, improving the overall score over the strongest baseline by 14.7--15.0% and increasing successful task completion on ColBench by up to 36.7%, showing that co-evolving the training system with the policy can substantially improve multi-turn RL.\nTitle: EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents\nAuthors: Min Cai, Dan Zhang, Gao Xing, Libin Ma, Zhaohui Xu, Xi Ye\nDate: Mon Oct 12 10:11:38 2026\n\nTraining LLM agents to interact with users over multiple turns is challenging because the learning signal depends not only on the agent, but also on the user simulator and environment, which jointly shape the agent's experience. Simulated users can be stochastic or inconsistent, while environments may contain unreliable tools, incomplete state coverage, and incorrect verifiers. Prior work mainly addresses the resulting instability through reward shaping or denser supervision, while treating these components as fixed. We instead introduce EAU, a meta-agent framework for environment--agent--user co-evolution. During training, EAU monitors trajectories, tool traces, rewards, and training statistics; diagnoses failures and uninformative interactions; and proposes targeted modifications to the user simulator, environment, reward design, and RL configuration. Validated changes are hot-swapped into the ongoing run, allowing the training setup to evolve together with the policy. These adaptations also persist across runs, so later training can build on previously discovered improvements rather than restarting from a fixed setup. We enable this process through an editable symbolic representation of user and environment dynamics, while the agent remains a neural LLM optimized with RL. Across three interactive-agent benchmarks and two backbone models, EAU consistently outperforms prior RL methods, improving the overall score over the strongest baseline by 14.7--15.0% and increasing successful task completion on ColBench by up to 36.7%, showing that co-evolving the training system with the policy can substantially improve multi-turn RL.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2610.16523] EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents (https://borowiecki.dev/abs/2610.16523)\nciteturn104search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 days ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; View a PDF of the paper titled EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents, by Min Cai and Dan Zhang and Gao Xing and Libin Ma and Zhaohui Xu and Xi Ye ... Cite as:  | arXiv:2610.16523 [cs.AI]\n\n# Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\n\n[Submitted on 12 Oct 2026]\n\n# Title:EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents\n\nAuthors:Min Cai, Dan Zhang, Gao Xing, Libin Ma, Zhaohui Xu, Xi Ye\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents, by Min Cai and Dan Zhang and Gao Xing and Libin Ma and Zhaohui Xu and Xi Ye\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\n> Abstract:Training LLM agents to interact with users over multiple turns is challenging because the learning signal depends not only on the agent, but also on the user simulator and environment, which jointly shape the agent's experience. Simulated users can be stochastic or inconsistent, while environments may contain unreliable tools, incomplete state coverage, and incorrect verifiers. Prior work mainly addresses the resulting instability through reward shaping or denser supervision, while treating these components as fixed. We instead introduce EAU, a meta-agent framework for environment--agent--user co-evolution. During training, EAU monitors trajectories, tool traces, rewards, and training statistics; diagnoses failures and uninformative interactions; and proposes targeted modifications to the user simulator, environment, reward design, and RL configuration. Validated changes are hot-swapped into the ongoing run, allowing the training setup to evolve together with the policy. These adaptations also persist across runs, so later training can build on previously discovered improvements rather than restarting from a fixed setup. We enable this process through an editable symbolic representation of user and environment dynamics, while the agent remains a neural LLM optimized with RL. Across three interactive-agent benchmarks and two backbone models, EAU consistently outperforms prior RL methods, improving the overall score over the strongest baseline by 14.7--15.0% and increasing successful task completion on ColBench by up to 36.7%, showing that co-evolving the training system with the policy can substantially improve multi-turn RL.\n\nSubjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nCite as:  | arXiv:2610.16523 [cs.AI]\n   | (or arXiv:2610.16523v1 [cs.AI] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2610.16523 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite (pending registration)\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Min Cai [view email]\n[v1] Mon, 12 Oct 2026 10:11:38 UTC (1,774 KB)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents · Pith (https://pith.science/paper/2610.16523)\nciteturn104search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 days ago; Crawled: yesterday; \"EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents.\" arXiv:2610.16523 (2026). https://pith.science/paper/EBSLUJDR ... See all figures in the PDF ↗ Figures reproduced from arXiv:2610.16523 by the authors.\n\n# EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents\n\nPaperPDF from arXiv\n\ndata files\n\nReviewThe objections and verdict, as JSON Paper breakdownThe authors' claims, conditions and measurements, as JSON Signed receiptProof this review has not been altered\n\nMin Cai, Dan Zhang, Gao Xing, Libin Ma, Zhaohui Xu, Xi Ye\n\nArtificial Intelligence · Submitted 12 Oct 2026 · 24 references\n\nPith's verdict: EAU proposes a meta-agent framework for co-evolving training components in interactive RL, with impressive empirical gains — but the comparison lacks matched compute budgets, making the headline improvements hard to attribute.\n\nT0 · AI review ▾\n\nHow this review was made: An AI referee (deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash) read the paper on October 14, 2026 and answered a fixed list of questions about it. T0 is the starting level every paper gets. The levels describe how deeply Pith has checked the paper, never the authors.\n\n  1. T0 AI review An AI read the paper and answered a fixed list of questions.\n  2. T1 Audit Automatic checks that the references and files the paper relies on exist and match.\n  3. T2 Compute The paper's code or calculations re-run with the same results.\n  4. T3 Formal statement The claims written out in a formal math language a computer can read.\n  5. T4 Proof Those claims proved, and the proof checked by computer.\n\nHow Pith checks papers →\n\n· 5 major objections · 6 minor · Ask about this paper · Read the full review →\n\nChallenge this review Comment (0) Read on arXiv ↗ Cite ▾\n\narXiv ID2610.16523 (version 1)\n\nPith IDEBSLUJDR, Pith's permanent record of this paper and its review\n\n[Button: Copy]\n\nCitation Dan Zhang, Gao Xing, Libin Ma, Min Cai, Xi Ye, Zhaohui Xu. \"EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents.\" arXiv:2610.16523 (2026). https://pith.science/paper/EBSLUJDR\n\n### The paper claims\n\nCo-evolving the training system with the policy substantially improves multi-turn RL for user-centric agents.\n\n### The reading\n\nTraining large language model agents to interact with users over multiple turns is hard because the learning signal depends on the user simulator and environment, which are usually fixed and can be noisy, inconsistent, or uninformative. The paper argues that instead of only improving the policy's reward signal, one should also adapt the simulator, environment, and training configuration during training. It introduces EAU, a meta-agent framework that monitors ongoing RL training, diagnoses failures, and proposes targeted modifications to these components, which are then hot-swapped into the live run. Across three benchmarks and two backbone models, EAU consistently outperforms prior RL methods, improving overall scores by 14.7–15.0% and increasing successful task completion on ColBench by up to 36.7%.\n\n### What carries the argument\n\nEAU (Environment-Agent-User Co-Evolution) — a meta-agent framework where a frontier coding agent acts as a meta-controller that continuously monitors RL training, diagnoses issues in the user simulator, environment, and training dynamics, and proposes programmatic modifications via editable symbolic interfaces. Simulated users can be stochastic or inconsistent, while environments may contain unreliable tools, incomplete state coverage, and incorrect verifiers. Prior work mainly addresses the resulting instability through reward shaping or denser supervision, while treating these components as fixed. We instead introduce EAU, a meta-agent framework for environment--agent--user co-evolution. During training, EAU monitors trajectories, tool traces, rewards, and training statistics; diagnoses failures and uninformative interactions; and proposes targeted modifications to the user simulator, environment, reward design, and RL configuration. Validated changes are hot-swapped into the ongoing run, allowing the training setup to evolve together with the policy. These adaptations also persist across runs, so later training can build on previously discovered improvements rather than restarting from a fixed setup. We enable this process through an editable symbolic representation of user and environment dynamics, while the agent remains a neural LLM optimized with RL. Across three interactive-agent benchmarks and two backbone models, EAU consistently outperforms prior RL methods, improving the overall score over the strongest baseline by 14.7--15.0% and increasing successful task completion on ColBench by up to 36.7%, showing that co-evolving the training system with the policy can substantially improve multi-turn RL.\n\nFigures\n\nImage: Figure 1 Figure 1. Illustration of how our meta-agent, EAU, manages RL training runs. EAU surpasses the prior SOTA on ColBench. Left: For the first three runs, EAU proactively restarts when it identifies issues that can only be resolved with offline patches. Middle: Run 4 and 5 displayed in blue and red are two full runs. Dashed lines indicate online, intra-run modifications. Run 4 explored individual modifications through local perturbations, whereas Run 5 used endpoint interventions followed by replication, reversal, and interaction tests. 1 arXiv:2610.16523v1 [cs.AI] 12 Oct 2026 Image: Figure 2 Figure 2. Illustration of a user￾centric agentic task. Showing 7 of the paper's 8 figures. See all figures in the PDF ↗ Figures reproduced from arXiv:2610.16523 by the authors.\n\nReferences\n\nWorks cited (24)\n\n  1. [3]\n\nZhenyu Hou, Yujiang Li, Jie Tang, and Yuxiao Dong. Single-rollout asynchronous optimization for agentic reinforcement learning.arXiv preprint arXiv:2607.07508,\n\nView on Pith Pith review arXiv\n\n  2. [4]\n\nURLhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2608.19880. Carlos E. Jimenez, John Yang, Alexander Wettig, Shunyu Yao, Kexin Pei, Ofir Press, and Karthik Narasimhan. Swe-bench: Can language models resolve real-world github issues?,\n\nView on Pith Pith review arXiv\n\n  3. [6]\n\nURL https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.07925. Fanqi Kong, Jiayi Zhang, Mingyi Deng, Chenglin Wu, Yuyu Luo, and Bang Liu. Infopo: Informat